#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-usb-devices.rst:171
#: a681c309761e4821bd4379340b03aeb0
msgid "Identifying controllers using the Qube Manager"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-usb-devices.rst:173
#: d639f258f4554478a6631649677bc5b1
msgid ""
"Using Qube Manager you can quickly determine the controllers on your system "
"and their BDF addresses, but not which controller a particular device is "
"attached to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-usb-devices.rst:175
#: 1f2d3ce76b9445a4b29a74a8aa17987f
msgid ""
"Open the Qube Manager, then right click on one of the qubes and open the "
"settings. Go to the tab “Devices”. Here you should see your available "
"devices along with their BDF addresses. Look for the lines containing “USB "
"controller”. They should look something like: ``01:00.0 USB controller: Name "
"of manufacturer``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-usb-devices.rst:177
#: 0d24296ed00c412ab693a24b07c4a4c6
msgid "The first part is the BDF address, in this example: ``01:00.0``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-usb-devices.rst:179
#: bea6aae083b24929ab329fb90cb48708
msgid ""
"If, for example, you have 2 USB controllers in your system because you added "
"one you should see 2 such lines and you can probably guess which controller "
"is the one on the mainboard and which one you added. For example, if you "
"have a mainboard with an Intel chipset, it is possible that all of the "
"mainboard devices show as “Intel Corporation”, while the added controller "
"shows another manufacturer’s name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-usb-devices.rst:181
#: e6cc47a811d146ac843952e1d3478db6
msgid ""
"Now you should be able to tell which is the BDF address of the mainboard USB "
"controller or the added USB controller."
msgstr ""
